To create the interactive user registration interface, you can use HTML for the layout, CSS for styling, and JavaScript for interactions such as form validation. Here's a simple example to get you started:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>User Registration Form</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
        }

        form {
            background: #fff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }

        input, textarea {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 10px 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }

        input[type="submit"] {
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }

        input[type="submit"]:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <form>
        <input type="text" id="fullName" placeholder="Full Name" required>
        <input type="email" id="email" placeholder="Email" required>
        <input type="tel" id="phone" placeholder="Phone Number" required>
        <textarea id="address" placeholder="Address" required></textarea>
        <input type="submit" value="Register">
    </form>
    <script>
        const form = document.querySelector('form');

        form.addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
            e.preventDefault();
            const fullName = document.getElementById('fullName').value;
            const email = document.getElementById('email').value;
            const phone = document.getElementById('phone').value;
            const address = document.getElementById('address').value;

            // Here you could perform server-side validation and submission
            alert(`Submitted: Name - ${fullName}, Email - ${email}, Phone - ${phone}, Address - ${address}`);
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This is a basic example. You can expand on this by adding further validations or additional fields. Keep in mind that in a production environment, you would want to handle form submissions in a server-side language like PHP, Python, Node.js, etc., for security and data storage purposes.
